{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III also known as Raymond the Old was the Count of Tripoli from 1136 until his death in 1152. He was a prince of the House of Normandy and a member of the powerful and influential Byzantine faction. He was the son of Bohemond I and Matilda of Hauteville and the brother of Bohemond II and Tancred of Hauteville.\n\n**Origins and Accession**\n\nRaymond was born around 1100 in Hauteville in southern Italy. He was the second son of Bohemond I and Matilda of Hauteville. At the age of two he was sent to Constantinople as a hostage to ensure the honor of his family. He later returned to help his brother Bohemond I conquer the County of Tripoli in 1109. Raymond became Count of Tripoli after his brother's death in 1136.\n\n**Marriage and Children**\n\nRaymond married Beatrice of Ibelin in 1137 the daughter of William of Ibelin. She was a niece of the Templar leader and Grand Master of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. This marriage strengthened his ties with the Templar order and helped to secure his position in the kingdom. They had several children together including Raymond IV of Tripoli and William of Tripoli.\n\n**Military Campaigns and Legacy**\n\nDuring his reign Raymond was involved in several military campaigns against the neighboring County of Sidon and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He died on October 15 1152 at the Siege of Montgisard. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV of Tripoli. He was remembered for his strong military and diplomatic skills as well as his role in establishing the County of Tripoli as a powerful and influential state in the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": " also", "logprob": -3.4103050231933594}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -0.00027056847466155887}, {"token": " as", "l